WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:04.820
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.120
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.420
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.660
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.480
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.460
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:20.620
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.080
Oh, hey there.

9
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.520
Today's episode is Andy's Ancestry.

10
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.620
What are we going to find out?

11
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.280
We're going to find out a lot.

12
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.760
It's Season 9, Episode 3.

13
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.900
This episode was written by Jonathan Green and Gabe Miller.

14
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.540
This is a new writer alert.

15
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:41.700
New writers.

16
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.580
Yes, new writing team alert.

17
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.240
They were a new writing team for this season.

18
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.340
Before joining The Office, they had written on The Class and Late Show with David Letterman.

19
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:58.440
And after The Office, they worked on The Cleveland Show, The Mindy Project, Superstore, and American Auto.

20
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.280
And they were really nice guys.

21
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:00.960
They were.

22
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.960
This episode was directed by Dave Rogers.

23
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.920
Anders, would you like a summary?

24
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.640
I really would because then I have some call sheet information for you.

25
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.160
Oh, I'm loving you and these call sheets.

26
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.480
You didn't read them when we were on the show.

27
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:14.940
I know.

28
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:15.480
No.

29
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.240
I literally, I stepped over them.

30
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.540
They would put them by our doors.

31
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.520
They would slide them under and I would be like, why are these papers?

32
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.280
I love a call sheet.

33
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:26.520
I know.

34
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.340
I would read them front and back.

35
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.600
I would study them.

36
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.320
Pam, I'm so excited you're into the call sheets, Ange, 20 years later.

37
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.700
Don't ever give up on yourself, people.

38
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.900
You have it in you to discover new things about yourself.

39
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.400
Here is your summary for this episode.

40
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.800
Nellie retaliates against Andy's bullying by falsifying his family tree.

41
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.000
Erin learns a new language in order to impress Andy and his family.

42
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.580
Pam helps Nellie practice driving for her upcoming driving teSt.

43
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.440
And Daryl helps Jim come clean with the secret that he's been keeping from Pam.

44
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.420
That summary is rich.

45
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:08.360
Yeah.

46
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.600
Angela, I have fast facts, but do you need to hit us with your call sheet delights first?

47
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.160
I would like to just set the temperature for the week for everybody.

48
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.500
Are you going to continue to tell us the weather?

49
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.000
Am I so old?

50
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.740
I'm like, how your parents just have the weather channel on all the time?

51
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:27.440
A little bit.

52
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.160
A little bit.

53
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:28.480
Okay.

54
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.860
Well, Steve Burgess, you know, you're my favorite fella who loves to take sunset photos by the lake.

55
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.000
He gave me all the call sheets for season nine.

56
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.340
I have all of them, lady.

57
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.780
It's very exciting.

58
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.920
I need everyone to know that the first day of filming happened on August 13th, 2012.

59
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.600
The high was 97 degrees.

60
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.760
Still real hot.

61
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.640
Day two, high of 100.

62
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.360
It continued into the high 90s for the rest of the week.

63
00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.200
The one other thing I want to share with you guys, I'm going to put this in stories as well.

64
00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:11.220
These call sheets for Andy's ancestry were so heavy with special announcements for all of the meetings they had to take for work bus the next week.

65
00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:12.260
Yes.

66
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.980
While we were shooting this, they were prepping that.

67
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:21.380
In fact, they just started putting buses on all the call sheets, like little images of buses.

68
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.340
Oh, that's very cute.

69
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.620
So, I'll share that in stories.

70
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:27.720
But, yeah.

71
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.780
And then I will have a few get-to-know-your-cast-and-crew moments coming up.

72
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:32.500
I love it.

73
00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:38.080
Well, fast fact number one, Angela, is that we had a very special guest star on this episode.

74
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:39.400
Randall Park.

75
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:40.060
Yes.

76
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.680
Is in our cold open.

77
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.080
You might recognize Randall from Fresh Off the Boat or WandaVision.

78
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.400
You know, the New Yorker did a fantastic profile on him last year.

79
00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.060
It's definitely worth a read.

80
00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.500
So, last year, Randall Park directed a film called Shortcomings.

81
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.780
It's based on the graphic novel by Adrienne Tomina.

82
00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:00.880
Okay.

83
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.980
You know that I went through a nerd out time in my life where I was super into graphic novels.

84
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.160
I love Adrienne Tomina so much.

85
00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.180
So, this whole New Yorker profile was kind of around this subject.

86
00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.040
So, anyway, give it a read.

87
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.000
It's very, very good.

88
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.660
I'll find it and put it in stories.

89
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:16.980
Oh, good.

90
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:17.620
Like a swipe up.

91
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:18.080
Yes.

92
00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:18.540
Yes.

93
00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:25.300
So, Randall also did an interview for NPR where he said that despite the popularity of Fresh

94
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:31.020
Off the Boat and WandaVision and being part of that world, the two things that he gets

95
00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:39.220
recognized for the most are for playing Kim Jong-un in the movie The Interview and for The Office.

96
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:40.820
Really?

97
00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:41.360
Yes.

98
00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.520
Those two things.

99
00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.880
Those are the things people yell out to him on the street.

100
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.400
But he said for a period of time, he actually forgot that he had been on The Office.

101
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.120
Well, yeah, because he's gone on to do so many things.

102
00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:54.480
Yes.

103
00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.240
And that people shout out to him on the street and he was confused.

104
00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.440
He actually told the story to Conan O'Brien and I have an audio clip.

105
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:03.920
Great.

106
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.600
You did a role on The Office and when I heard about this, I thought, oh, okay, well, maybe

107
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.040
you had an arc on The Office.

108
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.020
It wasn't an arc.

109
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.140
No, no.

110
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:15.780
You were barely in The Office.

111
00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:16.380
Yeah.

112
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.860
And they found that character.

113
00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:18.480
Tell us about that.

114
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.100
I mean, I did.

115
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.920
So, this was like many years ago.

116
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.920
It was the final season of The Office.

117
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:35.240
I got the chance to play a character named Asian Jim in like one scene during the final

118
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:35.680
season.

119
00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.680
And at that point, you know, the popularity of the show had been waning.

120
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.060
And, you know, I was in and out in an hour and I thought that was really fun.

121
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:46.360
And then I just kind of, I completely forgot about it.

122
00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:54.700
And then several years later, I'm like walking down the street and this car drives by and

123
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.180
a guy yells like Asian Jim and drives off.

124
00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:03.160
And I like, I'm thinking like, is this some like racist thing that I don't know about?

125
00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.460
Either you, either a fan just saw you or it's a hate crime.

126
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:13.000
I thought it was a hate crime, you know, because I had forgotten about my appearance on The Office.

127
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.840
And, you know, people would be walking up to me like, what's up, Asian Jim?

128
00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:17.960
I'd be like, f**k you.

129
00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.320
That's like, what are you, you know, and then.

130
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.200
By the way, I want to apologize for greeting you when you came here to the studio today.

131
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.000
You know, and you went like that.

132
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.520
Yeah, I did that and whatever.

133
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.560
I mean, man, I was way out of bounds.

134
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.660
Isn't that crazy?

135
00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:36.320
Yeah.

136
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.640
Have you ever done something that you forgot and then you were reminded later?

137
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.320
Are there roles like that?

138
00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.720
Oh, yeah, for sure.

139
00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.100
I was actually doing a Q&A at a university and there's a bunch of college kids.

140
00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:49.900
They were so wonderful.

141
00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.580
Everyone had questions about The Office.

142
00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.040
And then this one guy raised his hand and said, I don't have a question, just my favorite quote.

143
00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.120
So I thought it was going to be from The Office.

144
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.340
And he quoted my character from Hot Wives of Orlando.

145
00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.400
And I was clearly confused at firSt.

146
00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:05.140
I was like, what?

147
00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.320
And then he was like, Hot Wives of Orlando.

148
00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.180
I was like, oh, yes, right.

149
00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.120
Yes, I did that.

150
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:12.900
Well, we had a fan question from Claudia L.

151
00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:17.040
in Miami, Florida, who said, please tell us how it was working with Randall Park.

152
00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.920
You know what, Claudia, it's what he said.

153
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.860
We allotted one hour for that cold open.

154
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.200
He was in and out.

155
00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.360
I barely met him before I smooched him.

156
00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:36.500
You know, he was so nice, but it was just like a very kind of like professional moment, really.

157
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:37.660
It's so funny.

158
00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.500
And we shot those cold opens really quickly.

159
00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:41.300
We did.

160
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.660
And this cold open has become one of the most famous, most popular.

161
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.920
But for us, it was like one hour of our work day.

162
00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:54.340
We had no idea that it would take off the way it did or that Randall Park would take off the way he did.

163
00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:55.560
But I liked him.

164
00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.360
He was lovely and funny and ready to go.

165
00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.580
You know, all the best things.

166
00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:05.320
I'm so thankful I got to work with Randall again because I played Amy Chestnut in Fresh Off the Boat.

167
00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.720
And we had a few episodes together.

168
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.940
We had some great scenes where we were on a double date.

169
00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.380
And we were laughing so hard.

170
00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.780
And they let us improvise a little.

171
00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:16.520
And he is so fun.

172
00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.380
And I really enjoyed my time on that show and really enjoyed working with him.

173
00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.860
Are you ready for Fast Fact number two?

174
00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:24.740
I am.

175
00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:25.540
All right.

176
00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.100
This is a fan question from Selena P.

177
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:33.580
in Chandler, Arizona, who has a question about our Season 9 set.

178
00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:34.580
Okay.

179
00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:39.100
Selena said, whose decision was it to change the lighting of The Office?

180
00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:42.060
Season 9 is so much brighter than the rest of the series.

181
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:43.380
And I've always wondered why.

182
00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:45.440
What a good catch.

183
00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.060
Well, Selena, I reached out to Greg Daniels.

184
00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:54.220
And he said that Dave Rogers, our editor, and subsequently the director of this episode,

185
00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.820
had been complaining for a couple of years that the lighting seemed to be getting darker and darker.

186
00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:01.400
Really?

187
00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:02.480
Yes.

188
00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.140
And so they had a big meeting about it.

189
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:10.780
And they actually felt that the lighting this season was more of a restoration of our original look.

190
00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:13.020
And so good eye.

191
00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.460
They did bump up the lighting, Selena.

192
00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.840
I'm so impressed that you recognized this.

193
00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:19.940
I kind of want to do a side-by-side.

194
00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.220
Maybe we should.

195
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:21.740
Yeah.

196
00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:28.160
I'll also tell you, Selena, that after I had my baby, I got a bunch of melasma on my face,

197
00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.420
you know, the dark spots.

198
00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:35.140
And they were having a really hard time because the lighting in our office wasn't traditional

199
00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.640
kind of movie lighting where they light the actors from the front.

200
00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:40.060
It's not the pretty lighting.

201
00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.560
It's the overhead lighting.

202
00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.520
Everyone looks like they're a cryptkeeper or something.

203
00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:51.480
And it was actually that overhead lighting was enhancing these melasma spots on my face.

204
00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.760
And the makeup department literally couldn't put more makeup on my face.

205
00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.780
If you notice that Pam's makeup is a little heavier in season nine, it's because of this melasma.

206
00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:03.880
And actually bumping up the lighting helped with that.

207
00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.400
I had to do a bunch of test lighting days.

208
00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:10.540
They would combine certain makeup with certain lighting.

209
00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:18.400
And they ended up creating a little tiny, like, portable light box that would sit on my desk

210
00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:25.700
and shine on my face from below to help counteract the shadows from our overhead lighting system.

211
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.300
And then the makeup department tried a bunch of things.

212
00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:35.060
But, yeah, there was a bunch of new lighting stuff happening in season nine.

213
00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:35.400
Yeah.

214
00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:40.660
I mean, I know we don't want to go off on this tangent, but just the things women's bodies go through to have a baby.

215
00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:41.400
Yeah.

216
00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.960
The spots on the face that have never gone away from me.

217
00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.060
They've lightened now over the years.

218
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.800
But, you know, then I had another baby and then hello again.

219
00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.260
And then where's my little box lamp?

220
00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:54.480
Yeah.

221
00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.420
At that point, I was off the office.

222
00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:58.060
I travel with a box lamp.

223
00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:58.920
I wish.

224
00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:00.020
All right.

225
00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.860
Fast fact number three.

226
00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.740
I'll have you know this episode was very well received by critics.

227
00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:10.300
The new critic over at the AV Club, Eric Adams, gave it a B.

228
00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:13.180
I did not see any comments from Miles.

229
00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:14.860
I looked through all the comments.

230
00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:15.320
Okay.

231
00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.180
Nothing yet.

232
00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.320
What about a Steve B?

233
00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:19.060
Didn't see a Steve B.

234
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:19.530
Okay.

235
00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.900
Mark Trammell of TV Equals named it, quote,

236
00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.660
the best episode of the final season of The Office to date.

237
00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.380
Well, I thought that was nice.

238
00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.220
But, Mark, it's only the third one.

239
00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:32.980
It's a big proclamation.

240
00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:33.760
It is.

241
00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.700
And after this episode aired, E!

242
00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:39.720
Online said that Pete, a.k.a.

243
00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:45.740
Jake Lacey, was, quote, especially charming and named him one of the best things in pop

244
00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:46.840
culture this week.

245
00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.500
Let me tell you, people had their eye on Jake.

246
00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:50.140
Yep.

247
00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:51.280
He was going to be a superstar.

248
00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:57.180
I got curious about what other things were on the best things in pop culture this week,

249
00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.160
along with Jake.

250
00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.380
You want to hear some of them?

251
00:11:59.600 --> 00:11:59.820
Sure.

